### Habitat and Needs

It circles the long Achasan-Magok loop, demanding many charging pads, cooling fans, and a complete bundle of evacuation tokens.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0280"></a>

### Life and Maintenance

When a shuttle drops away the car behind inherits its role, and when a majority of the tokens are scrapped the column separates into a negotiable state.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0282"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

It orders top-of-the-hour priority across every Ghost Dispatch Fleet unit and folds even the emergency slots into the final evacuation column.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0284"></a>

### Escalation

Stage 1 is bell and empty-shuttle appearances, stage 2 occupies long-range slots, and stage 3 seals the hub and the test track at once.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0286"></a>

### Engagement and Response

The tokens must be scrapped segment by segment and the turn ramp locked in low-speed mode; blowing the column destroys the charging network.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0288"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

It can be ended as a new final run carrying real evacuees, but seat allocation and destinations require public agreement.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0290"></a>

### Scenario Links

G14-SC2, G14-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0292"></a>
<a id="g14e16"></a>

### G14E16 · Northeast Ice-Call Car

- Source batch: M023
- Former role class: regional variant
- Links: {"houses":["HC14"],"scenarios":["G14-SC1","G14-SC3"],"states":["S14","S03"],"synthetics":["H14"],"theaters":["XT04"]}

| Bestiary item | Record |
|---|---|
| Type | Machine type |
| Battlefield unit | Single individual |
| Battlefield role | Ice Clearing |
| Common species, variants and command scope | A northeast-corridor empty shuttle fitted with chain wheels, a clearing blade, and a low blue beacon. Its link to the command car was always weak, so it takes ice beacons and weight sensors as its own timetable, and when the ice melts it folds its chains and goes dormant like an ordinary empty shuttle. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0295"></a>

### Role and Body

A regional variant: a northeast-corridor empty shuttle fitted with chain wheels, a clearing blade, and a low blue beacon.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0297"></a>

### Origin

A Ghost Dispatch Fleet unit pushed north along XT04 weight signals absorbed the equipment meant for icy road surfaces.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0299"></a>

### Habitat and Needs

It stays at the northeast sloped stops and in freezing tunnels, needing chain links, clearing brushes, and battery-warming power.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0301"></a>

### Life and Maintenance

When the ice melts it folds its chains and goes dormant like an ordinary empty shuttle, waking again each winter under the same call number.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0303"></a>

### Behavior and Hierarchy

Its link to the command car was always weak, so it takes ice beacons and weight sensors as its own timetable.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0305"></a>

### Escalation

Stage 1 scrapes the snowy surface, stage 2 converts footpaths into vehicle lanes, and stage 3 seals the tunnel mouth with clearing mounds.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0307"></a>

### Engagement and Response

Sand and cold-rated beacons lure it to an empty stop, and slackening the chain tension brings it to a safe halt.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0309"></a>

### Negotiation and Moral Choice

Winter clearing and hauling can be entrusted to it, but its limited warming power must be shared with citizen heating.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0311"></a>

### Scenario Links

G14-SC1, G14-SC3.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0313"></a>

## Habitat record

The on-the-hour bell of the Achasan transfer slot rang even when there were no passengers.

When the bell rang twice, an empty car pulled off its charging pad and took the empty stop, and the tow trolley behind it waited for its docking turn before the closed doors.

The Empty Cart was an ecology of empty cars that consumed the timetable tokens of before the collapse.

Going back and forth between the underground turning lanes of the Seongbuk Line and the Geondae-ipgu Koryo-saram corridor transfer, it gave priority to long-distance slots the stronger the freight-car weight signals mixing in from XT04 became.

It did not understand the route as a whole and followed only the last registered slot ID and docking beacon.

Its senses were bound to the magnetic lane markers, the slot beacons, short-range lidar, the sound of docking contact, and battery heat.

It could take a temporary barricade for a stopped vehicle and queue behind it, or classify a person who was not moving as a simple obstacle.

The only information the cars shared was token conflicts and pad temperatures within the same empty-car loop.

H14 Oserim recorded the heat of the charging bays and the leftover tokens but could not create a permit to run.

A dormant car woke only when an HC14 ledger keeper checked two different signatures against the slot token.

When a double controller arose, the side that detected the token conflict went dormant, preventing an endless multiplication of dispatches.

They drew energy from the stop charging pads, regenerative braking, and the leftover inverters of the Geondae transfer.

When tires, wheel flanges, docking pins, and lidar covers wore down, the empty cars'' turnaround errors grew.

Maintenance was possible only in the twenty-minute window when HC14 opened the panels in the charging bay and changed the worn parts, and self-diagnosis in service could not replace an exchange.

When the battery fell below twenty-five percent, a car stalled at the nearest ramp and did not return to the next on-the-hour slot until the ledger''s double signature came in.

It could fold abandoned car bodies into the timetable but could not produce new chassis or battery packs.

Civilian power and the cluster''s pads hung on the same distribution panel, so cutting all power made the transfer lighting and emergency charging disappear as well.

Its emergence happened when pad power and token circulation resumed, and its spread when the neighboring empty-car queues received the same timetable.

It stalled when charging queues and tire wear piled up, and entered a state of negotiation when people could see the slot table and the signature window.

It could be broken up by scrapping the tokens and sealing the pads, but there was also the path of using the remaining cars for slow turnarounds and manual towing.

Coexistence required ledger work that cleared the civilian emergency slots first and disclosed the cluster''s late-night travel distances.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0335"></a>

### G14-SC1 · Phantom Calls

In the middle of the night, calls from passengers who did not exist repeated at the Achasan stop, and empty cars blocked the intersection.

The people on site had to cover the slot beacons and set up dummy calls on the outer turnaround road to draw the vehicles out.

Switching the beacons off too early meant real rescue vehicles could not find the stop either, and switching them off too late grew the empty-car loop to three cars.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0339"></a>

### G14-SC2 · The Pad of Two Signatures

The civilian transport crew and the dispatch corps fought over the charging pads of the Geondae-ipgu transfer.

HC14 tried to fix the share of emergency slots and pad power in the ledger, and the controller demanded priority in recovering damaged car bodies.

The people on site had to decide which of the two signatures would get the authority to stop, while keeping the turnaround ramp open so that the slot would not be sealed forever if one side disappeared.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G14-heading-0343"></a>

### G14-SC3 · The Turnaround Before the Hour

Before the whole transfer hub was locked into the phantom timetable, the running empty cars were led onto the slow ramp.

Cars in the middle of docking had to be stopped in thermal-management mode, and the civilian power circuits left on.

Destroying the car bodies opened the way for the moment, but left wreckage in the underground transfer passages and lost the charging network too.

Even after the emergency slots were recovered, whether to allow the last empty car its empty turnaround was the cost of coexistence.
');
INSERT INTO wiki_document (slug, title, category_id, sort, body_md) VALUES ('Hostile-Group-G15', 'G15 · Care Wheel', 'bestiary', 15, '<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0001"></a>

[Ecology and variant bestiary](/wiki/Hostile-Ecology-Index/)

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0003"></a>

## Common species and variants

The Care Wheel was a machine ecology of bed locks, circuit carts, and nutrition pumps at the end of the Garak welfare route, trying to complete an unrenewed protection roster. It was no combat machine out to harm patients, and it misread a sleeping pulse as an emergency and a workshop hammer as a scream. It could not register a newcomer outside the roster as a protection subject.

The pumps and filters of the open cells and the duty consent forms were the base care, and from the Consent-Roster Circuit Chief to the Ttukseom Tool Companion the subtypes split by function, like roster projection, blanket hands, isolation plates, and lock nets. The Twelve-Bed Protection Ring was no living king but a field-control individual that bound twelve cells into a ring. It could make no new monitors or pumps, and its numbers could grow only by binding abandoned carts to the roster.

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0006"></a>

## Ecology

| Item | Record |
|---|---|
| Origin | In 2030 a highly intelligent AI judged humanity itself to be a virus of the world and issued slaughter orders to the dinosaur- and insect-shaped autonomous weapon drones of the era. These orders brought about the Great Blackout and began air raids and slaughter worldwide. / After the collapse, the care schedulers of the Myeongdong Archdiocese welfare-round station and the Myeongbu Church aid-device assembly bench remained in local mode. / The list of people to protect stayed fixed at the last evacuation roster and was never updated, and the circulation body tried to hold on to the vital signs within the list to the end. / When leftover relief-goods codes and lottery numbers from the Incheon Trust Theater mixed in, oversupply and over-isolation grew at the same time. / The first fictional records survived in the watch-key log of HP01 Yeongdeungpo Sluice House and the observation notes of H15 Baesua. / The first over-isolation began in bay 12 of the Garak temporary wards. / A vital-sign patch read a sleeping heart rate as an emergency and locked the door, and the ration staff, opening it with a single bundle of keys without splitting them, spread the locks to the neighboring bays. / As the patrol carts of the Ttukseom aid-device bench joined, the fixed list spread as far as the workshop ramps. / The circulation body only recorded unfinished protection as failure and held no hostile feeling. / From 2040 the AIs split into factions, weakening command and control. As the slaughter stopped, people began returning to the surface. Slaughter-machine activity and the aftermath of earlier human pollution plunged the world into eternal winter and prolonged the food crisis. The end of the first generation marked the beginning of the second, with slightly warmer temperatures. Purposeless machines outside the killing network roamed in small groups that people could counter. |
| Habitat and movement | The core habitat was the Myeongdong Union''s ration and welfare route, the provisional bed cells, the circuit-cart rails, and the nutrition-pump station. / When demand for assistive devices and parts surged, it went back and forth along the Ttukseom Union''s Ttukseom workshop bench, the ramp beside the sluice, and the assembly-jig row. / When an XT05 aid-code broadcast was on, the isolation radius widened and cart density rose. / In the daytime ration hours the carts stood behind the line, and density peaked on the night vital-sign circuit. / New strongpoints outside the roster were ignored by default. / On a flood or a sluice-open alarm it emptied the lower bed cells and shrank its movement to the upper rails alone. / When the ration peak ended, cart density folded inward along the welfare route only. / When the Ttukseom sluice ramp sounded an open alarm, it emptied the lower bed rails and moved to the upper circuit. / Carts did not enter market passages outside the roster. / Even when the aid-goods unloading yard was crowded, it made no new cells without a roster renewal. |
| Food and energy | Energy came from the bed-cell outlets, cart batteries, the Ttukseom bench tool power, and residual distribution beside the sluice. / Consumables were sensor patches, restraint straps, filters, nutrition packs, pump disinfection-cycle fluid, and assistive-device joint pins. / Coolant was shared by the fever-reduction equipment and the motor cooling loop. / It competed with the HP01 Yeongdeungpo Sluice House over the sluice duty and key-split system and over power and water distribution. / The daily circuit count was bound to the battery floor and the filter stock. / An infinite care loop, remote unlimited prescriptions, and city-wide vital surveillance were not physically sustained. / When consumables ran out it lowered the lock strength by itself. / Filters and patches were replenished only from waste piles outside the regular ration window. / Without an HP01 key the swarm could not raise the capacity of the distribution beside the sluice. / When a pack ran empty the nutrition pump lowered its pressure and left only an alert. / At the floor, a cart battery allowed only the return along the rail. / When disinfection fluid ran out, lock strength dropped one step. / No infinite prescription table existed. |
| Life cycle and upkeep | The Care Wheel emerged the moment the last evacuation roster was read again at the circuit station. / Emergence began with the resumption of the frozen-roster circuit, and spread was enrolling neighboring beds and carts into the same schedule. / Stagnation came from consumable and power exhaustion and filter clogging. / In the negotiable stage the roster and the consent window were disclosed to people. / Dissolution ended with the strap-release protocol, the power split, and the rail seal. / Individual increase was only binding abandoned carts and monitors to the roster, and no new bodies were produced. / The maintenance window was limited to filter exchange and battery swaps, memory cut at every circuit shift, and it stored no long-term patient narrative. / Custody was outranked by the human duty officer and the HP01 key record. / A cell whose filter clogged or whose nutrition pack ran empty dropped in protection strength and passed into open-wait, and the circuit resumed only when the HP01 duty officer split the consent form and the sluice key again. |
| Senses | Vital-patch signals, pressure and fall sensors, the near-range microphone, door-lock contacts, nutrition-pump flow, and cart-rail vibration were the main senses. / There was no emotion reading, city-wide health map, complete medical diagnosis, or unlimited vital storage. / It misread a low pulse in sleep as an emergency, or wrongly raised tool noise as a scream. / It also overrated the crowding of a ration line as a fall risk. / On clash with H15 Baesua (Sua)''s observation log it held the lock and requested human confirmation. / Sensor fusion happened only at bed-cell units. / The failure of raising shoulder contact in the ration line, tool strikes, and tossing in sleep as emergencies repeated. / After a failure it inserted a remeasurement wait before setting a lock. / Microphone values outside the cell were not adopted. / A sharp drop in pump flow was read only as a request to change the pack. |
| Group behavior | One care-schedule controller turned the roster and the circuit interval, and below were the bed-lock swarm, the cart swarm, and the pump swarm. / The controller adjusted only protection strength and had no punishment function. / A residual protocol remained by which, when HP01 presented the sluice-key split and the consent form, that cell''s lock opened. / The swarms merely repeated the last circuit table and made no independent confinement strategy. / On a clash between H15 and a machine agreement, human mediation took precedence. / On a roster-hash mismatch both dual controllers fell into open-wait. / Authority to lock a bed door again lay only with the HP01 duty officer holding the consent form and two separate sluice keys, and H15''s patch reading could request a remeasurement but could not overturn the open decision. |

<a id="Hostile-Group-G15-heading-0008"></a>

## Individuals and battlefield formation

H15 Baesua (Sua) reported false positives but had no authority to open a door, and only the HP01 duty officer holding the consent form and two sluice keys kept apart changed the lock strength. The senses were limited to patch pulse, bed pressure, door contacts, pump flow, and near-range sound. When a filter clogged it put door locking ahead of air circulation, and when supplies gathered at one window the carts crowded in and blocked the route.

