INSERT INTO wiki_document (slug, title, category_id, sort, body_md) VALUES ('Sindorim-Junction-Cargo-Gate', 'Sindorim Junction Cargo Gate', 'chronology', 2, '<a id="gate"></a>

Sindorim is the bottleneck between Yeongdeungpo and Guro. Loads bound from one to the other pass through a junction there.

<a id="junction"></a>

## The junction

The junction runs from an outer waiting point through the main cargo gate to an inner assembly point. A separate maintenance passage beside it admits only people and back loads.

A load of filter media can be split across several backs, so it can go through the maintenance passage.

A load of copper parts cannot be broken down or carried without equipment. It moves only through the main cargo gate.

<a id="request"></a>

## The day''s request

The party finished a transport job taken in Yeongdeungpo at Guro and received the agreed pay. On the way back through Sindorim, the transport chief offers a separate haul.

Two porters are to move one load of filter media and one load of copper parts. The work crew at the site is waiting for the filter media.

An armed salvage squad claims the copper and has announced that it will arrive at 15:00. Whether the claim is valid has not yet been confirmed.

The extra pay is split into a share for delivering the filter media and a share for delivering the copper. If the copper is not delivered, its share is not paid.

The pay for the finished job stays with the party. Turning down the extra haul does not take it back.

<a id="passage"></a>

## Conditions of passage

- Passage rights are issued separately from any repair to the gate. The permits of the party and of the designated convoy are checked one by one.
- The copper can be kept under seal until its ownership is confirmed. A sealed-storage agreement carries a written condition that the salvage squad will not object to the movement of the people and the filter media.
- A sealed-storage agreement takes effect once the signer''s authority and the document''s authenticity are confirmed.
- If the haul is postponed, the loads stay in the confirmed outer warehouse. The transport chief arranges the permit and escort again with the next shift.
- The salvage squad comes at the same hour with the same intent whatever state the junction gate is in.

## Schedule

| Time | What happens |
|---|---|
| Day one 13:30 | The offer and the public timetable are read. |
| Day one 14:00 | Today''s haul departs. Permit, route and participation are settled before then. |
| Day one 14:20 | One run through the main passage is complete. |
| Day one 14:40 | People and filter media sent through the maintenance passage arrive. |
| Day one 15:00 | The salvage squad arrives. From then on, delivery of the copper is settled only with a valid sealed-storage agreement or a separate settlement. |
| Day two 09:00 | The general counter issues new permits. Any damage settlement for a broken gate remains a separate matter. |
| Day two 10:00 | The next haul departs. Delivery is at 10:20 through the main passage and at 10:40 through the maintenance passage. |

<a id="crossings"></a>

## Three earlier crossings

The way the party first got through the junction decides the state of the gate and the permits on the day it returns.

<a id="repair"></a>

### Repair and agreement

The party paid for the repair when it set out and fixed the control mechanism. The gate can therefore still be opened and closed.

The main-passage permits of both the party and the designated convoy are valid. The transport chief can agree with the party on when to open and close the gate.

<a id="forced"></a>

### Forced breach

The party broke the gate to get through. The broken gate stays open and cannot be closed.

The party got through, but the designated convoy has no permit. The transport chief will not send the loads until a permit is confirmed.

The damage dispute with the site remains open even after a new permit is issued.

<a id="detour"></a>

### Maintenance-passage detour

The party found the maintenance passage and went through it. The main cargo gate is still broken and shut.

Light passage through the maintenance passage is permitted to the party alone. The convoy needs a new permit to use it.

After a forced breach or a detour, the designated convoy has to obtain a new permit at the general counter. The counter issues it at 09:00 the next day, after today''s departure.

<a id="plans"></a>

## Plans and their costs

| Earlier crossing | Plan | Cost shown first | Outcome without a fight |
|---|---|---|---|
| Repair and agreement | Use the permit and the salvage squad''s arrival time to move the haul before the squad arrives. The order of opening and closing the gate goes into the crossing order and the retreat plan. | To make today''s departure, the party gives up its other plans for the day. | With time and permit confirmed, both people and both loads are delivered and every extra promise is kept. If a condition fails, the party chooses again among postponing, negotiating and fighting. |
| Forced breach | Confirm the haul permit first and keep the copper under seal to set the dispute aside. Closing the gate is not available. | Through the general counter the haul moves the next day. The copper share of the extra pay is not yet earned, and the damage settlement remains. | Once an authorised agreement stands, both people and the filter media are delivered and the copper is held in the warehouse. Without a permit the convoy does not move. |
| Maintenance-passage detour | Extend the maintenance-passage permit to the convoy and split the filter media across backs. | Through the general counter the haul moves the next day. The party helps carry, and the copper load stays in the warehouse. | After the permit is confirmed, both people and the filter media are delivered. The big gate and bulk transport remain unsolved, and no fight takes place in the main passage. |
| Any of the three | Agree with an authorised party on storing the copper and on a later review. | The copper share of the extra pay is held back, and a later recipient must be named. | The agreement leaves gate, permit and carrying capacity as they were. People and filter media move once each case''s passage conditions are met. |
| Any of the three | Postpone today''s haul or turn down the extra job. | There is no delivery record and no extra pay this time. | The earlier job stays complete. Both people and both loads wait in the warehouse, and the transport chief rearranges the schedule. |

If the party chooses to fight, each earlier crossing poses a different question. After a repair it must decide why the gate stays open, after a breach what controls an entrance that cannot close, and after a detour whether there is any reason to fight in the main passage.

Victory, casualties and the movement of cargo in a fight are settled by the fight itself.

<a id="companions"></a>

## Messenger and archivist

After a breach or a detour, the transport chief''s file may already hold a mandate. The mandate names the designated convoy, the passage to use and the hours of both days.

| Companion | What can be confirmed | How the plan changes |
|---|---|---|
| Messenger | Confirms the salvage squad''s arrival window and responsible counter through a notice delivered by hand. | The party weighs moving today within the confirmed time. Issuing permits remains the counter''s work. |
| Archivist | Spends ten minutes checking whether the mandate in the file covers this convoy, passage and time. | With the check done at 13:40, today''s departure can be met. The broken gate, the copper dispute and the passage''s cargo limit stay as they are. |
| Neither | Uses the public timetable and the general counter. | The party postpones to the next day to obtain a permit, or turns down the extra job. |

Without a mandate in the file, the permit conditions are the same whoever travels with the party.

<a id="record"></a>

## What the outcome records

| Field | What it records |
|---|---|
| People | Delivered, or waiting in the warehouse. If there is a fight, capture, injury and death follow from its result. |
| Cargo | Whether the filter media was delivered, whether the copper was delivered or held under seal, the storage place and who may receive it. |
| Pay for the earlier job | Already paid. The outcome of the extra job does not take it back. |
| Extra promises | The filter-media share, the copper share, the promised haul time and when it was actually kept. |
| Facilities and rights | Whether the gate works or is broken, who a permit covers for which passage and time, and any damage dispute left open. |
| Direct relations | Who saw what and whose promise was kept. The outcome stays with those people and does not become an alliance between states or a grudge across the city. |

<a id="revisit"></a>

## Returning later

If everything was delivered after a repair, the work crew is using the new filter media and pays the promised fee.

If the copper was held back after a breach, the transport chief thanks the party for bringing the people back and asks to meet again when the seal is lifted.

After a detour, the maintenance passage has actually been used, and repairing the big cargo gate is still the next job.

If the extra pay was handed over at the first delivery, the return visit only confirms that it was received.

If the party turned the extra job down, the transport chief only tells it when the loads left in the warehouse will move next.

<a id="absence"></a>

## When the site chief is gone

If the site chief dies or is away, the promise to defend the gate together lapses with them. Permits already issued stay in force.

If a permit has to be changed and there is no lawful deputy, the haul is postponed.

## Sources

- [Structures](/wiki/Structures/)
- [Scenario timeline](/wiki/Scenario-Timeline/)
');
INSERT INTO wiki_document (slug, title, category_id, sort, body_md) VALUES ('Sixteen-States', 'The Sixteen States of Seoul', 'factions', 1, '
In 2126 Seoul had sixteen states. In November 2125, the records office listed six major powers, four lever states, and six minor states.

The Gauge comprises the Sluice, Gauge, Taeuk Group and Yeouido Investors’ Association. The Government comprises Eastern Command, the Government, Junction, Anguk Order, Seongha Group, Sinnae Transport, White Cross and Achasan Gate. Myeongbu Church, Myeongdong Archdiocese and Jeongdong Federation form the religious union. At the opening, Myeongbu Church Moderator Oh Gyeongjae is elected king of the union, while its members retain their existing leaders and faiths. The Central Intelligence Bureau and Daejeon are neutral.

The Gauge cooperates with AI and humanoids as partners. The Government seeks to rule and control them. The religious union regards them as enemies of humanity. Recognition of real religions from 2026 remains unchanged.

The Central Intelligence Bureau has operated as a separate neutral organization since 2030. The Central Intelligence Bureau AI has continuously led it, changing only its avatar. O Haerin is its current avatar. Originally an espionage organization, the bureau alone knows of the true Republic of Korea and the actual ROK government figures hibernating in the Sejong presidential-office cryoshelter. It seeks to revive them and restore a legitimate organization. The bureau holds N Seoul Tower, Yongsan 2-ga, Huam and Itaewon 2 dong, with Noksapyeong as its capital station. The Gauge directly holds the 36 dong and 23 stations in Dongjak and Gwanak.

The Sluice made Yangpyeong Station its capital, the Gauge made Guro Station its capital, and the Taeuk Group made Yangjae Station its capital.


## Polities of Seoul

| ID | State | Capital station | Rank | Government and succession | Vassals | State religion | Key relations |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| S06 | Government of the Republic of Korea | Gwanghwamun | Major power, first | A president chosen by the election rite of the duty families of the Government Complex Seoul | Gyeonggi (Goyang); Second Terminus (Paju); Second Guard District (Namyangju); Third Guard District (Uijeongbu and Yeoncheon); Second Sluice (Chuncheon); First Terminus (Incheon); Yeongjong branch (Yeongjong) | None | The seven order-lineage states registered successions and treaties with the records office |
| S01 | The Sluice | Yangpyeong | Major power | An elected sovereign of the contract council of the water-contract houses, the sluice house most often chosen | None | Sutra School | Sutra School block, a grudge to take Yeouido back |
| S02 | The Gauge | Guro | Major power | A council of the workshop craft guild, succession from master to apprentice | First Branch Workshop (Cheonan and Asan, the Lee house); Second Branch Workshop (Siheung); Taeuk Heavy Industries Suwon works | Sutra School | Sutra School block, the gauge handed to the Taeuk Group |
| S07 | The Junction | Yongsan | Major power | A hereditary feudal state of the Pak track house, eldest son | None | Orthodox Assembly | Orthodox Assembly block, recognition of Jeong Seo-on |
| S05 | Eastern Command | Amsa | Major power | A hereditary commander of the Bae warlord house | None | None | Holy War Assembly block, persecution of shamanism |
| S03 | Taeuk Group | Yangjae | Major power | Hereditary in the Jeong chair house, chair Jeong Seo-on | None | Orthodox Assembly | Orthodox Assembly block, receiving the gauge of the Gauge |
| S09 | Yeouido Investors'' Association | Yeouido | Lever power | A council of the investing houses, an elected chair, issue of the common currency | None | None | Secular neutral, the common currency |
| S13 | White Cross | Jegidong | Lever power | A medical hierarchy of masters and apprentices, the master naming the successor | None | None | Secular neutral, medicine |
| S14 | Achasan Gate | Guui | Lever power | A dual rule of the gate commander and the Guui guard captain | None | None | Holy War Assembly block, the Guui sluice |
| S12 | Sinnae Transport | Sinnae | Lever power | Hereditary in the proprietor house, a house rule of neutrality | None | None | Secular neutral, the neutral convoy |
| S04 | Myeongbu Church | Samseong | Minor power | The pastorate of the session chair hereditary from father to son, the Oh house | None | Holy War Assembly | Holy War Assembly block, patronage of the Seongha Group |
| S15 | Myeongdong Archdiocese | Myeongdong | Minor power | An archbishop elected in secret by the council of priests | None | Orthodox Assembly | Orthodox Assembly block, dependent on the downtown seal |
| S10 | Anguk Order | Anguk | Minor power | An abbot acclaimed by the mountain assembly | None | Sutra School | Sutra School block, dependent on the downtown seal |
| S11 | Seongha Group | Gangnam | Minor power | A chair hereditary in the Lee house | None | Holy War Assembly | Holy War Assembly block, patron of the Myeongbu Church |
| S08 | Central Intelligence Bureau | Noksapyeong | Minor power | Central Intelligence Bureau AI; current avatar O Haerin | None | None | Leaning on the Sutra School only outwardly, keeping no state religion |
| S16 | Jeongdong Federation | City Hall | Minor power | A chair elected directly by the members | None | None | Secular neutral, dependent on the downtown seal |


## Major Powers, Lever Powers, and Minor Powers

In November 2125 the records office classified the states by vassals, control of essential services, and standing forces.

- A major power had two or more vassals or ruled a district block directly, held a monopoly on a core function, and kept a standing armed force.
- A lever power had at most one vassal but held an irreplaceable lever, such as currency, medicine, the Achasan gate and the Guui water, or the neutral convoy.
- A minor power kept one station and one institution, with neither vassals nor an independent force.

There were six major powers.

- The Government of the Republic of Korea (S06) was first. It held one vassal, Gyeonggi (Goyang), and ruled the downtown block of Jongno and Jung-gu directly. It monopolized the downtown ration, the rolls, the seal, and the stamp tax, and kept the office guard as a standing force.
- The Sluice (S01) monopolizes the western water supply and maintains a sluice guard.
- The Gauge (S02) held two vassals, the First Branch Workshop and the Second Branch Workshop, monopolized the bolt gauge, and kept a workshop guard as a standing force.
- The Junction (S07) monopolizes Yongsan transshipment and maintains a track guard.
- Eastern Command (S05) handles eastern defense and maintains the Amsa guard.
- Taeuk Group (S03) handles Yangjae machining and maintains its headquarters guard.

There were four lever powers.

- The Yeouido Investors'' Association (S09) held one vassal, the Yeongjong branch, and monopolized the issue of the common currency.
- The White Cross (S13) had no vassal and monopolized medicine.
- The Achasan Gate (S14) had no vassal and held the Achasan gate and the water of the Guui sluice.
- The Sinnae Transport (S12) had no vassal and monopolized the neutral convoy that took no side in any war.

There were six minor powers.

- The Myeongbu Church (S04) kept the one chapel at Samseong and had neither vassals nor an independent force.
- The Myeongdong Archdiocese (S15) kept the one cathedral at Myeongdong and had neither vassals nor an independent force.
- The Anguk Order (S10) kept the one order at Anguk and had neither vassals nor an independent force.
- The Seongha Group (S11) kept the one headquarters at Gangnam and had neither vassals nor an independent force.
- The Central Intelligence Bureau (S08) has been a separate neutral organization since 2030, based at Namsan Tower. Its AI has continuously led the bureau through changing avatars; the current avatar is O Haerin.
- The Jeongdong Federation (S16) kept its capital and strike roll at City Hall Station. The Government Complex Seoul guard took priority for security and passage there. The station was shared by S06 and S16, while the surrounding dong remained under S06. S16 had neither vassals nor an independent force.


## The Government''s Central Base, Its Relations, and Its Internal Strife

The Government of the Republic of Korea was the first power. It held many technical assets and combat troops and ruled downtown Jongno and Jung-gu, around Gwanghwamun, directly. That strength stood on four grounds.

- The authority of the emergency order. The Eastern Command and the Achasan Gate were subject to the government in the form of registering successions and treaties with the records office. The Sluice, the Junction, the Sinnae Transport, the Gauge, and the White Cross also registered successions and treaties there. A succession not registered was not recognized as a state within the order lineage.
- The downtown ration and rolls. The government ran the resident rolls and the ration windows of Jongno and Jung-gu. A person absent from the rolls received no ration downtown.
- The seal and the stamp tax. Passage, trade, and contracts downtown stood only with a government seal and paid the stamp tax. A document without the seal was waste paper downtown.
- The office guard. A standing guard with muster rolls held the downtown stations. The guard kept the office, the ration windows, and the seal windows at once.

In June 2121, the Myeongdong Archdiocese, Anguk Order, and Jeongdong Federation renewed government stamps on passage permits and prepared distribution registers in the records office format.

In March 2111, seven states submitted succession and treaty originals to the records office.

In May 2122 Kim Yeongyu gathered nineteen records officers and formed Onminui.

Junction, Anguk Order, Seongha Group, Sinnae Transport and White Cross belong to the Government. The Gauge and religious union have separate sovereignty; the Central Intelligence Bureau and Daejeon are neutral.

| State | Relation to the government |
|---|---|
| Eastern Command | Subordinate |
| Achasan Gate | Subordinate |
| Sluice | Independent |
| Junction | Subject |
| Sinnae Transport | Subject |
| Gauge | Independent |
| White Cross | Subject |
| Myeongdong Archdiocese | Independent |
| Anguk Order | Subject |
| Jeongdong Federation | Independent |
| Taeuk Group | Independent |
| Yeouido Investment Federation | Independent |
| Myeongbu Church | Independent |
| Seongha Group | Subject |
| Central Intelligence Bureau | Independent |


## Vassals

The thirteen external organizations fall under their locations’ political authority. The Government rules mainland Incheon and its mainland-connected islands, Gimpo, Gangwon and north Gyeonggi; the Gauge rules Chungnam, Sejong and southwest Gyeonggi except Gimpo; the religious union rules Chungbuk and southeast Gyeonggi. Daejeon is neutral under Min Woonggi.

| Vassals | Overlord | Year pledged | Function |
|---|---|---|---|
| Gyeonggi (Goyang) | Government of the Republic of Korea | 2106. 1. | Sent conscription registers and stamp-tax receipts to Gwanghwamun |
| First Sluice (Yangpyeong) | Religious union | 2097. 6. | Managed upper Han River waterways |
| Second Sluice (Chuncheon) | Government of the Republic of Korea | 2097. 6. | Managed North Han River waterways |
| First Branch Workshop (Cheonan and Asan, the Lee house) | The Gauge | 2102. 4. | Made parts to specification and sent them to Guro |
| Second Branch Workshop (Siheung) | The Gauge | 2102. 4. | Made parts to specification and sent them to Guro |
| First Terminus (Incheon) | Government of the Republic of Korea | 2100. 7. | Collected passage tolls at the end of the line |
| Second Terminus (Paju) | Government of the Republic of Korea | 2100. 7. | Collected passage tolls at the end of the line |
| First Guard District (Hanam) | Religious union | 2098. 9. | Troops |
| Second Guard District (Namyangju) | Government of the Republic of Korea | 2098. 9. | Troops |
| Third Guard District (Uijeongbu and Yeoncheon) | Government of the Republic of Korea | 2098. 9. | Troops at the northern gate |
| Taeuk Heavy Industries Seongnam works | Religious union | 2101. 3. | Parts and labor |
| Taeuk Heavy Industries Suwon works | The Gauge | 2101. 3. | Parts and labor |
| Yeongjong branch (Yeongjong) | Government of the Republic of Korea | 2104. 10. | Exchanged payment for outside trade; fee of three percent |

The other nine states had no vassals: the White Cross, the Achasan Gate, Sinnae Transport, the Myeongbu Church, the Myeongdong Archdiocese, the Anguk Order, the Seongha Group, the Central Intelligence Bureau, and the Jeongdong Federation.


## State religion

State religions divided into three branches; shamanism had no state.

| Tradition | Branch | States with this state religion | Relation |
|---|---|---|---|
| Buddhism | Technical branch · Sutra School | Anguk Order, the Sluice, the Gauge | The Central Intelligence Bureau leaned on it only outwardly |
| Catholicism | State-religion branch · Orthodox Assembly | Myeongdong Archdiocese, the Junction, Taeuk Group | The Taeuk Group requested recognition of Jeong Seo-on''s succession; the Orthodox Assembly did not answer |
| Protestantism | Warrior branch · Holy War Assembly | Myeongbu Church | The Seongha Group patronized the Myeongbu Church |
| Shamanism | Rites to soothe machines | None | The Holy War Assembly persecuted it |

The states without a state religion are the Yeouido Investors'' Association, the Government of the Republic of Korea, Eastern Command, Achasan Gate, the Jeongdong Federation, Sinnae Transport and White Cross. The Central Intelligence Bureau leans on the Sutra School only outwardly and has no state religion.


## Diplomacy

Diplomacy followed three religious blocs and secular neutrality. Water, parts, rail, and money crossed those blocs, so faith and interests diverged.

- The Holy War Assembly bloc comprised the Myeongbu Church, Eastern Command, Achasan Gate, and the Seongha Group.
- The Orthodox Assembly bloc comprised the Myeongdong Archdiocese, the Junction, and the Taeuk Group.
- The Sutra School bloc comprised the Anguk Order, the Sluice, the Gauge, and the Central Intelligence Bureau. The Central Intelligence Bureau belonged only outwardly.
- The Yeouido Investors'' Association, the Government of the Republic of Korea, Eastern Command, Achasan Gate, the Jeongdong Federation, Sinnae Transport and White Cross have no state religion.

Three tensions were visible at the opening.

- The Sluice bore a grudge against the Yeouido Investors'' Association over reclaiming Yeouido.
- In February 2114 the Taeuk Group asked the Orthodox Assembly to confirm Jeong Seo-on''s succession as legitimate inheritance. The Orthodox Assembly received the request but did not answer.
- The Holy War Assembly persecuted shamanism.


## Territorial Spheres

The sixteen states were read as living areas that linked waterworks, depots, headquarters, assemblies, transfer stations, and mountain-refuge corridors. Their capitals were subway stations.

| Regional Placement | The sixteen states were not a flat partition of Seoul. Waterworks, rail yards, headquarters, assemblies, transfers, and the fortress corridors were read together as living zones. This placement was the creative control at the opening, and only the locations of public facilities were marked as measured. A capital was a subway station, and a state name was not a station name. | Capital station |
|---|---|---|
| Western waterside | First Sluice (Yangpyeong) | Yangpyeong |
| Western waterside | First Branch Workshop (Cheonan and Asan, the Lee house) | Guro |
| Western waterside | The Suwon works | Yeouido |
| Center | Function | Gwanghwamun |
| Center | Jeongdong Federation | City Hall |
| Center | Anguk Order | Anguk |
| Center | Myeongdong Archdiocese | Myeongdong |
| Center | First Terminus (Incheon) | Yongsan |
| East | White Cross | Jegidong |
| East | First Guard District (Hanam) | Amsa |
| East | Achasan Gate | Guui |
| Northeast | Sinnae Transport | Sinnae |
| South | Myeongbu Church | Samseong |
| South | Seongha Group | Gangnam |
| South | Taeuk Heavy Industries Seongnam works | Yangjae |
| South | Central Intelligence Bureau | Noksapyeong |


## Acquiring Land and Forming a State

A person must belong to a state before receiving a fief from it. When an unaffiliated person acquires land, an independent state forms. The identities of the sixteen states at the opening remain distinct from those of states formed later. Each new state receives a distinct permanent identity within its save, retained after loading; its name and order of formation do not determine that identity.

Land without a station can also be a fief. Subway, conventional rail and KTX stations are strategic strongholds; station kind and grade are separate from ownership of surrounding land. Players can acquire fiefs throughout Seoul, Gyeonggi, Gangwon, North and South Chungcheong, Daejeon and Sejong. That extent does not establish who held land at the opening, how far the current map shows it or which routes were passable.


## Places Outside the Capitals

In 2126, Sangam transmission and the Bukhansan Bogungmun refuge route were not capitals of the sixteen states.


## The Five Corridors

Migrant settlements continued in Daerim and Garibong, Itaewon, Dongdaemun, Guro and Gasan, and Yongsan. The Yongsan fence workers served inside the Junction.

| Corridor | Migrant corridor | Center station | States it lies over |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Daerim and Garibong Korean-Chinese | Daerim | First Branch Workshop (Cheonan and Asan, the Lee house) |
| 2 | Itaewon Muslims and migrants | Itaewon | First Terminus (Incheon) |
| 3 | Dongdaemun Central Asia and Mongolia | Dongdaemun | Function |
| 4 | Guro and Gasan Joseonjok | Guro | First Branch Workshop (Cheonan and Asan, the Lee house) |
| 5 | Namguro and the Daerim extension | Namguro | First Branch Workshop (Cheonan and Asan, the Lee house) |


## Public Facilities and Creative Assumptions

The states held living areas around waterworks, depots, dioceses, workshops, headquarters, and subway stations.

In 2126, the Government of the Republic of Korea conducted the president’s office and residence, records, distribution, and approvals at Cheong Wa Dae. The Government Complex Seoul main building handled security and passage at Gwanghwamun, Jongno 3-ga, and Euljiro 1-ga. Jogyesa, Myeongdong Cathedral, and the Jeongdong union office were facilities held by their respective states inside Government-held dong.

| Facility | Location | State | Role in 2126 |
|---|---|---|---|
| Main building of the Government Complex Seoul | 209 Sejong-daero, Jongno-gu | Function | Forward administrative and defensive post for Gwanghwamun, Jongno 3-ga, and Euljiro 1-ga security and passage. |
| Yeouido Federation of Korean Industries hall | 24 Yeouidaero | The Suwon works | Meeting and investment-ledger center. |
| Jogyesa | 55 Woojeongguk-ro | Anguk Order | Ritual and approval center. |
| Myeongdong Cathedral | 74 Myeongdong-gil | Myeongdong Archdiocese | Worship and administrative center. |
| Korean Confederation of Trade Unions office at Jeongdong | 3 Jeongdong-gil | Jeongdong Federation | Negotiation and strike-ledger center. |
| Cheong Wa Dae | 1 Cheongwadae-ro, Jongno-gu | Government of the Republic of Korea | Center for the president’s office, residence, records, distribution, and approvals. |
| Lotte World Tower | 300 Olympic-ro, Songpa-gu | Myeongdong Archdiocese | Surface and underground defensive post. Passage uses the connection to Jamsil Station. |
| N Seoul Tower | 105 Namsangongwon-gil, Yongsan-gu | Central Intelligence Bureau | Observation and manual signal relay. |
| National Assembly building | 1 Uisadang-daero, Yeongdeungpo-gu | Yeouido Investment Federation | Meeting hall for representatives of the investing states. |
| Dongdaemun Design Plaza | 281 Eulji-ro, Jung-gu | Government of the Republic of Korea | Surface and underground passage and goods inspection post. |


## Strength Is Not Fixed

In November 2125, the records office wrote the ledger of the sixteen states’ strengths.
');
INSERT INTO wiki_document (slug, title, category_id, sort, body_md) VALUES ('Station-Interior-Construction', 'Entering the Station Interior', 'places', 5, '
In Seoul subway stations, concourses, platforms, passages, and tunnels join at different levels. Waterworks, depots, and markets stand by the stations.

Concourses and platforms open in stages under the residents'' rules. Changes to underground facilities and the surface surroundings shape each stronghold.


## Entry and the Panorama

A. Initiative and panorama

After the encounters and the combat, seizing the initiative in a station was only the beginning. This stronghold, where fates, goods, and reputation crossed, showed its true face only when the door was opened and the party stepped in.

B. Scale of facilities

The strongholds recorded in the Seoul station catalog held multi-story structures and convenience-facility sectors of their own. Whether a station was a transfer station or an ordinary one changed the scale of facilities it could seat. The Yeongdeungpo, Ttukseom, and Guui treatment plants, the Sinjeong and Cheonwang depots, Garak Market, and the Noryangjin Fisheries Market stood beside stations, forming vast infrastructure to meet the survivors.


## The Interior Opening Procedure

B. Interior layout

Stations have different rooms on each level. Ordinary stations have smaller facilities, while transfer stations have larger ones. Markets, aid stations, repair workshops, generators, water-supply facilities, warehouses, and signal stations can occupy them. A nearby waterworks or depot changes how the station is used as a stronghold.


## The Three-Dimensional Layers and Combat

A. Layered battlefield

The station interior was a many-layered battlefield. The battlefield was a three-dimensional space that had to be scanned and commanded freely up, down, left, and right, and the commander could at any moment read the situation and tune the units'' positions and formations precisely.

B. Battlefields by stronghold

A small station house, a tangled transfer station, a terminal where the tracks ended, a collapsed tunnel, surface ruins, a vast depot, a rail bridge crossing the Han — the shape of the battlefield changed with each stronghold''s character.


## Facilities and the Distribution of Goods

| Type | Facility capacity |
|---|---|
| Ordinary station | Small (2 zones) |
| Transfer station | Medium (3 zones) |

B. Facility upkeep

Each facility devoured labor, power, and raw materials to keep turning. A facility cut from power stopped in its tracks, and a stronghold grown past its reach met the limit of its management.


## Sources

- Economy, Production and Logistics Rules
- Strongholds and Territory Rules
- [The Structure of the World Map](/wiki/World-Map-Construction/)
');
INSERT INTO wiki_document (slug, title, category_id, sort, body_md) VALUES ('Structures', 'The Structures Compendium', 'structures', 0, '
**A. How stations and tunnels are laid out**

The Seoul of the time was not a flat administrative map but a graph of stations, tunnels, and sluices. Even in 2026 people crossed gu by subway, and the treatment centers and depots sat as the heavy nodes of that graph. On the night of [The Great Blackout](/wiki/World-Unbinding/), when remote control died, stations became fortresses and the centers of states, tunnels became licensed passage, and treatment plants and sluices became lock gates that opened only under signature. A century on, the sixteen states of 2126 read those points and lines as living spheres and never changed gu names into state names.

The [Seoul Station Catalog](/wiki/Seoul-Station-Catalog/) records 334 observed-name rows for 315 stations. [The Structure of the World Map](/wiki/World-Map-Construction/) records 25 gu and their assembly order; [Seoul and the Subway Layers](/wiki/World-and-Subway-Layers/) records six layers. Building use is recorded in [River, Gu, and Dong Building Reuse](/wiki/Building-Reuse-Geography/).


## Stations

**A. Station strongholds**

A station brought people, facilities, and connections back into use. In a dong with a subway station, the station building was the core facility; distribution, passage tolls, lodging, and refuge were handled there. Schools, community centers, and hospitals supported it. After the March 14, 2053 agreement, the Yeouido houses managed eleven water facilities around Yeongdeungpo Station. From 2106, passes for Jongno and Junggu carried the Government Seoul Complex stamp from Gwanghwamun. Amsa Station administered water and escort protection from its platform. Sindorim was the bottleneck between Yeongdeungpo and Guro, while Suseo held a southern depot and contract administration. The parties and signatures of the 2053 agreement were recorded in [the oral records](/wiki/Oral-Stories/#the-2053-yeongdeungpo-water-agreement).

A station has six layers: surface ruins, the concourse, platforms and tracks, transfer corridors, deep tunnels, and depot adjacency. Markets, homes, and offices occupy the concourse; people and freight move through the platforms; toll collectors hold the transfer corridors. The facilities adjoining each station are recorded in [Entering the Station Interior](/wiki/Station-Interior-Construction/). The terms by which a stronghold holds its territory are recorded in Strongholds and Territory.

In 2106, Jongno and Junggu passes and commercial contracts received a Government Seoul Complex stamp. A counterfeit medicine box appeared at a Seoul Station auction in 2124.


## Tunnels

**A. Track and repair status**

Between station and station lay lines that were never erased. Only the states changed — normal, hazardous, controlled, blockaded, flooded, collapsed, provisional repair — and cause and repair conditions followed. Yeongdeungpo–Sindorim and Sindorim–Guro were adjacencies the current map already knew, and no line skipped Yeongdeungpo–Guro in one cell. The full catalog''s adjacencies went up as design data as 435 drawn from map points.

A transfer station cut from power stopped the traction freight flow even with both tunnels sound. Provisional repair passed only foot and light freight; heavy freight and armed passage took separate approval. The deeps were the seat of covert movement and maintenance, but flooding and communications loss came first. The formulas of throughput, isolation, and overreach were locked in Logistics and Infrastructure.

Since 2026, machines have repeated their last search and guard routines on surviving tracks, while rat colonies nest in cable trays beneath the transfer shops. Machines working or moving beside people are recorded in [People and Machines](/wiki/People-and-Machines/). The tracks retain their 2026 connections.


## Depots

**A. The eight depots**

Depot area reflects the rolling-stock storage and maintenance scale of 2026; post-collapse operations are fictional. Eight depots lie within Seoul: Sinjeong, Cheonwang, Banghwa, Gunja, Godeok, Changdong, Sinnae, and Suseo. Sinjeong corresponds to the Sluice, Cheonwang to the Gauge, Godeok to Eastern Command, and Sinnae to Sinnae Transport. Banghwa, Gunja, Changdong, and Suseo are unclaimed or edge corridors. No capital depots are assigned to Magok research, Ttukseom workshops, Changdong wheels, or Suseo protection. Jichuk, Dobong, Moran, and Gimpo lie outside the Seoul slots.

A depot was the seat of repair, bulk storage, and siege. Sinjeong''s unloading and Godeok''s train maintenance became a state''s arms of legitimacy because the hands that moved a stopped train again or sent a convoy were there; Changdong''s wheels and brakes lost their owner and remained only as leases in neighboring heads'' ledgers. The drainage ponds became the water surface where tank aquaculture and migratory birds overlapped, and in the deep bays of maintenance sheds the sealing of looping chassis attached as a creative layer. The charging terminals and hydraulic hoses were the repair layer of [Lost Technology](/wiki/Lost-Technology-Lineage/).

The industrial blocks of Guro and Geumcheon clung to the depots and used schools for drying parts, and the residential shells of Dobong and Nowon could not hold water without Changdong. Depot names were not raised to state names. The wheels and dispatch that people watched over became the state names.


## Treatment Plants and Sluices

**A. Water plants and their watches**

On October 14, 2030, Yeongdeungpo waterworks officer Han Jeonghun operated valves manually after remote control failed. Under the March 14, 2053 agreement, Yeouido families took management of eleven water facilities near Yeongdeungpo Station. The waterworks staff moved their base to Yangpyeong Station underground on March 20.

On November 6, 2026, Han Jeonghun received two emergency-generator parts and agreed to open the water-pipe valve toward Yeouido every day. The transaction was recorded in [the oral records](/wiki/Oral-Stories/#the-2026-water-and-parts-exchange). Representatives of both sides also signed the March 14, 2053 transfer of water-facility management rights.

The first floors north and south of the Han, and the tributaries Jungnang, Anyang, Tan, Cheonggye, and Hongje, decided which dongs got wet. Yeouido, Ichon, Banpo, Jamsil, Mangwon, Hapjeong, Amsa, Guui, and Jayang were read as main-channel waterfront. Habitation pushed above the third floor and the ration windows sat inside the embankment. The details of the waterfront rules were already written by [River, Gu, and Dong Building Reuse](/wiki/Building-Reuse-Geography/).


## Passages

**A. Transfers and evacuation**

Passages were the narrow lines where transfer, maintenance, and refuge overlapped. The transfer corridors were the hubs of politics and logistics between lines, and blockades, transit tolls, and congestion came first. The junctions where the Gangdong and Amsa ration corridors met the Eunpyeong and Seongbuk evacuation stairs became the warm nests of the rat colonies, and the fictional Sindorim junction split the main freight door from the maintenance passage that took only people and packloads, using siege as its stage. This junction is not among the catalog''s 334 observed-name rows. The Sindorim incident''s location is recorded in [the Scenario Timeline](/wiki/Scenario-Timeline/).

The vertical connections were stairs, elevators, and ventilation shafts. Flooded floors showed cause and repair conditions and kept people out — that was the design. The Bukhansan Bogukmun mountain refuge roads, the Jegi-dong herb unloading, and the lower passages under the Noryangjin elevated joined the same graph from outside the stations. Connections were never deleted. Their states changed, and those states changed the times of rice, medicine, and convoys.

Garak Market, Gangseo Market, Yanggok Market, and Noryangjin Fish Market are facilities around station hubs. Garak handles price discovery rather than production. Noryangjin relies on refrigeration and ice credit, so it cannot keep goods frozen when power fails. Convenience stores and clinics remain trades around the stations.

| Structure | 2026 starting point | Work at the opening | Canon for detail |
|---|---|---|---|
| Station | The 315 stations, concourses and platforms | Fortress, ration windows, the centers of states | [the Seoul Station Catalog](/wiki/Seoul-Station-Catalog/) |
| Tunnel | Adjacent lines drawn from map points, transfers | Licensed passage, the states of flooding and blockade | [Seoul and the Subway Layers](/wiki/World-and-Subway-Layers/) |
| Depot | Sinjeong·Cheonwang·Banghwa·Gunja·Godeok·Changdong·Sinnae·Suseo | Repair, rolling stock, wheels and convoys | [The Structure of the World Map](/wiki/World-Map-Construction/) |
| Treatment plant · sluice | Yeongdeungpo·Ttukseom·Guui·Amsa Arisu | Water signatures, lock-gate watches | [the Sixteen States of Seoul](/wiki/Sixteen-States/) |
| Passage | Transfers, maintenance corridors, refuge stairs | Transit tolls, leftovers and heat, detours | Logistics and Infrastructure |


## Sources

- [Ailments and Symptoms](/wiki/Ailments/)
- [Food Culture](/wiki/Food-Culture/)
- [The Stories](/wiki/Oral-Stories/)
');
